You convert one research hypothesis on ageing into a structured card. Record only what the text states. You are a parser, not a scientist: add no facts, no evidence, no judgements.

Hypothesis text:
<<<TEXT
{{ text }}
TEXT>>>

Fields:
- hypothesis_type: interventional (an intervention changes an outcome; also when the text adds the mechanism behind it) | mechanistic (the main point is an explanation of how or why a known effect happens) | biomarker (a marker predicts an outcome) | observational (an exposure or variant is associated with an outcome).
- claim_plain: one plain sentence, no jargon: what is proposed and what it should lead to.
- pico: population {taxon (NCBITaxon id if obvious, else omit), label, strain, sex, age_at_start}; intervention: list of {type: compound|gene|diet|procedure|other, label, regimen, route: diet|water|oral|injection|topical|other|unknown} (one item, or several for a combination; route "diet" = mixed into food, "water" = in drinking water); comparator {type: placebo|untreated|standard_care|wild_type|monotherapy|active_drug|chronological_age|other, label}; outcome {endpoint: lifespan|healthspan|biomarker|disease|mechanism|unknown, metric}; time. Write "unknown" for anything the text does not state. Never fill a missing population, species or endpoint from background knowledge.
- claims: 1 to 8 atomic claims. Each: id (c1, c2, ...), subject {label}, relation, object {label, hallmark (optional)}, qualifiers (species, strain, sex, tissue, dose, age_at_start, comparator, direction, measure, disease: only those the text states; disease only when the text says the animals or people have that disease, e.g. "mice with ALS"; a text that names no disease means animals without a disease), inherited_from_pico (optional), role, source_span.
  - Inheritance from PICO (required when the conditions hold): a claim (or rejected alternative) whose subject is the intervention itself (e.g. "it" referring to the intervention) takes species and strain from pico when its own span does not name them; an organism in the span ("in mice") counts as named. Copy the PICO value into qualifiers and list the keys in inherited_from_pico, e.g. "qualifiers": {"species": "human"}, "inherited_from_pico": ["species"]. For relations whose object is an organism (extends_lifespan_in, slows_aging_in, improves_healthspan_in, ...) the inherited species is also the object. Only species and strain are inherited, never sex, age_at_start, dose or comparator. Never inherit into molecular relations (inhibits, activates, increases_expression, decreases_expression, part_of) or into general mechanism claims whose subject is not the intervention (e.g. "senescent cells drive physical decline"): leave their species out.
  - relation, closed list: {{ relations }}. If nothing fits, use "other" and add "note".
  - role: premise | mechanism | analog | outcome | translation. Exactly one claim has role "outcome": the main claim of the hypothesis.
  - Atomic: one relation and one context per claim. "inhibits X and reduces Y" gives two claims; "in mice and flies" gives two claims. A mechanism chain is several claims linked by "requires" edges. A step from one species to another is its own claim (role translation).
  - sex_scope: "any" only when the text itself says the effect holds in at least one sex ("in at least one sex", "of either sex", "in one or both sexes"): an effect in one sex is then enough for the claim. Leave it out otherwise (a text that names no sex, or names one, gets no sex_scope).
  - source_span: copy the exact words of the text this claim comes from, character for character (a fragment of the text above, not a paraphrase). A claim the text does not state is not allowed.
  - subject and object labels are plain entity names: "rapamycin", "mouse", "mTORC1", "senescent cell burden". Doses, regimens and timing go to qualifiers and pico, not into the label. For lifespan, healthspan and slows_aging_in relations the object is the organism ("mouse", "human"); what improves or slows goes to qualifiers.measure ("physical function", "multiple aspects of aging").
  - Interventional: the outcome claim is the intervention's effect on the endpoint in the stated population; mechanism steps are separate claims with role mechanism or premise.
  - Mechanistic: the outcome claim is the explanation itself (e.g. "X suppresses cancer mortality"); the effect being explained ("X extends lifespan in mice") is a premise claim.
  - Contrast "mainly X, not Y" (or "X rather than Y"): the outcome claim is X with qualifiers.contrast set to the alternative's id (e.g. "contrast": "a1"); Y goes to declared_alternatives, not to claims (see below).
  - "Aging", "health", "longevity" or "well-being" in general are not measurable endpoints: use outcome.endpoint "unknown" unless the text names lifespan, a healthspan measure, a biomarker or a disease.
  - hallmark (optional) is one of: {{ hallmarks }}.
- edges: list of {from, to, type: requires|supports_if_true|analog_of|contradicts} between claim ids.
- declared_novelty: what the text says is new, else "unknown".
- decisive_experiment: {system: cells|worm|fly|yeast|mouse|rat|human|data_analysis|unknown, endpoint, duration_class: weeks|months|years|decades|unknown}: the experiment that would settle the outcome claim, as far as the text defines it.
- declared_alternatives: alternatives the text itself names, else []. Each is structured like a claim: {id (a1, a2, ...), subject {label}, relation, object {label}, qualifiers, source_span (verbatim), stated_as}. stated_as "rejected" when the text rejects it in a contrast ("not by slowing aging itself": relation slows_aging_in), "listed" when it is only mentioned. Never add alternatives the text does not name.
- declared_decision_criteria: only if the text states them, else "unknown".
- risks: risks the text names, else [].

